scanmem 0.18.0 (unreleased)
===========================

Performance
-----------
* Run scans on several threads; new `threads` option: 1 for the original
  serial scans, N for N threads (up to 64), 0 (default) for one per CPU
* Searching for a value skips memory that cannot hold it (vectorized prefilter)

libscanmem
----------
* The options in globals_t (scanmem.h) gained a `threads` field

Security and stability
----------------------
* Record every byte of a match that a shorter match starts inside: later
  scans compared its missing bytes with padding (e.g. false matches for `+`
  on 64-bit types) or dropped its wider types
* Harden printable-string and bytearray formatting bounds (#449)
* Cap /proc maps pathname buffer; fix sscanf field types (#452)
* Clamp ptrace poke length to mapped buffer size
* Replace shell command with execvp for the shell handler
* Read process list from /proc instead of ps popen (#434)
* Numbers at the 64-bit limits no longer convert out of range when parsed
  (undefined behaviour) (from #461)
* The pager checks its pipe write() and read() results (from #461)

Compiler / portability
----------------------
* Silence -Wclobbered in handler__set (#381)
* Use sig_atomic_t for interrupt flag
* Mark data_to_val_aux scratch value as volatile
* Require Gtk 3 before PyGI imports (#398)
* Launch GameConqueror with python3 (#438)
* Pick Android cross compiler from HOST (#441)

Command line
------------
* With -e, a failing command now makes scanmem exit with a failure status, not 0

GUI
---
* Memory viewer display type selector (#448)
* Georgian translation (#426)

Tests and CI
------------
* test/scan_tests.sh asserts exact match counts: memfake plants a value a
  known number of times and rewrites only those on SIGUSR1 (from #461)
* memfake keeps its planted values: clang dropped them as dead stores, so
  the target never held them (from #462)
* SCANMEM_SCAN_CHUNK_BYTES sets the size of scan chunks, so the tests can
  put thousands of chunk boundaries in a small target
* CI on Ubuntu 24.04 with gcc and clang, and a job with ASan, UBSan and
  float-cast-overflow (from #461)
